+- **details:**
+  - Surface 1 -- locales/README.<code>.md (OPEN, owns `ja`): all 32 carry `<!-- source-digest: README.md sha256:886c5e27060e7b30 -->`, which matches neither the current normalised 16-hex (`3e0623c938fcd514`) nor 64-hex (`3e0623c938fcd514ceac6c8614ebc74be76ff6e09cd68d680a995811e1cae474`) digest of README.md. Structure still matches (11 headings, 4 fences), so the delta is prose, not shape. The canonical kitchen holds mirrors for `ru`/`et`/`ded` only (all three stale) and none for `ja`, which phases/translate.md names as this role's drift ownership. Translating the current README.md into ja (and re-checking the uk case) is the next package; it is why SAIT-004 is `blocked` rather than `ready`.
+  - Surface 2 -- desktop/README.<code>.md delta, why the doc surface was not clean: the English source had gained two sections (`### qBittorrent`, `### Fonts: named, never installed`) and one table row (`| `qbittorrent` | ... |`) since the 1b166ae6a7cf8a5c translation round -- 15 headings -> 17, 14 table rows -> 15 -- which is exactly what a stale digest is for. The Sections were inserted structurally (immediately before each locale's own Electron-apps heading, verified by the `Electron` anchor in heading 10), the table row directly after each locale's `obs` row, and its third cell copied byte-identically from that same locale's `obs` row so the "survives an app update" phrasing cannot drift inside a file. The digest was restamped to `b77c16d423936045` (the current normalised 16-hex of desktop/README.md; that file contains no `\d+.\d+.\d+` version string, so normalisation is a no-op on it).
+  - Owned locale count is 29 of 32 by rule (phases/translate.md: Core owns en/ru/et/ded; ja and uk are the dedicated producer's, and are in the mandatory-six default set, so both were kept fully current here). The 29 mirror files became 68 keys; `LanguageLabel` was deliberately left as Core's `Language` literal that SAIT-002 recorded as the convention, and my first attempt to localise it was reverted for exactly that reason.
+  - Core-owned drift, reported and not touched: desktop/locales/ru.json is 67/68 keys (missing `LanguageLabel`); desktop/locales/{et,ded}.json are 49/68 (missing 19). Because `T($key)` has no English fallback, the Russian GUI currently renders the literal string `LanguageLabel` next to the language dropdown, and the Estonian/Deda GUIs render 19 raw key names. desktop/README.{ru,et,ded}.md are still at 15 headings / 14 rows / digest 1b166ae6a7cf8a5c, and kitchen/README.{ru,et,ded}.md are stale against README.md. These are Core's four languages; this role only reports them.
+  - Tooling defect found while establishing the digest rule (Core/repo, outside this role's write scope): the canonical check in saipen/tools/validate.py section 1b11 computes `_want` as the full 64-hex digest but compares it against a 16-hex marker captured by `([0-9a-f]+)`, so it can never match -- the translation-stale signal is unsatisfiable by construction, and it reads the legacy `.saipen/saitranslate/kitchen` path rather than the canonical `.saipen/extensions/subs/saitranslate/kitchen` the payload moved to in SAIT-003. Markers in this package therefore follow the shipped 16-hex convention and document the rule (`sha256(\d+.\d+.\d+ -> VERSION)`, first 16 hex) rather than inventing a third format.
+  - Also reported: README.md links its language switcher to `locales/README.<code>.md` and the tree has all 32, while the canonical kitchen's root-level `README.<code>.md` mirrors cover only ru/et/ded -- so a collect of those three would either create files whose names the switcher does not use or overwrite the shipped `locales/` copies with a differently-named surface. Core should decide which of the two shapes is authoritative before collecting that group.
+  - Boundary clean: every write in this pass is inside .saipen/extensions/subs/saitranslate/ (kitchen + scratch + its own memory). No main-tree file was written, no commit, no push. The scratch harnesses (backfill-*.py, apply*.py, verify*.py, render-check.ps1, docdelta-*.py) live under this role's own directory and are reproducible evidence for every number above.
